---
// Teacher guide: the lab-forms -> Canvas loop for the CSP course.
// Not linked from student navigation; linked from README.md.
// Excluded from student search.
import Base from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import {
  MCQ_SETS,
  MCQ_BANK,
  BIG_IDEA_RANGE,
  bigIdeaShare,
  questionsForSet,
  questionId,
} from '../../data/mcq-bank';

const LETTERS = ['A', 'B', 'C', 'D'];

/** The 48 clinic questions, grouped, for the key table below. */
const KEY_SETS = ([1, 2, 3] as const).map((set) => ({
  set,
  meta: MCQ_SETS[set],
  rows: questionsForSet(set).map((q, i) => ({
    n: i + 1,
    id: questionId(q, i),
    ced: q.ced,
    answer: LETTERS[q.answer],
    correct: q.choices[q.answer],
    trap: q.trap ?? '',
    explain: q.explain,
  })),
}));

const MIXED_SHARE = bigIdeaShare(MCQ_BANK.filter((q) => q.set !== 2));
const ALL_SHARE = bigIdeaShare(MCQ_BANK);

const SAMPLE_JSON = [
  '{',
  '  "format": "apcsp-lab-answers/1",',
  '  "student": "Ada Lovelace",',
  '  "lesson": "4.5",',
  '  "lessonTitle": "The Full State Machine: Patrol, Chase, Attack",',
  '  "downloadedAt": "2026-11-17T16:02:11.402Z",',
  '  "answers": [',
  '    {',
  '      "id": "4.5:sc1:q1",',
  '      "block": "socratic",',
  '      "question": "The enemy jitters because the flip condition...",',
  '      "answer": "..."',
  '    },',
  '    {',
  '      "id": "4.5:dc1:q1",',
  '      "block": "decompose",',
  '      "question": "Falling out of the chase if means it returns...",',
  '      "answer": "..."',
  '    }',
  '  ]',
  '}',
].join('\n');
---
<Base title="Collecting Lab Answers via Canvas — AP CS Principles" active={undefined} root=".." docsHome="../docs/index.html" css="../style.css"
  description="Teacher workflow: students answer lab questions on the lesson pages, download a JSON, and upload it to Canvas.">
<div class="page" data-pagefind-ignore>
  <nav>
    <a href="../index.html">Home</a>
    <span class="sep">/</span>
    <span class="here">Teacher: Lab Forms + Canvas</span>
  </nav>

  <h1>Lab Forms + Canvas</h1>

  <p>
    Every reflection question in the lessons (socratic checkpoints and bug hunts,
    where a lesson keeps one) has an answer box, injected at build time by
    <code>src/lib/formify.ts</code>. Answers save on the student's device
    as they type, and each lesson has a
    <strong>Download answers (.json)</strong> button that produces one
    small, self-describing file — the question text, the student's
    answer, and a stable question id (like <code>4.5:sc1:q2</code>) all
    travel together. This page is the whole loop for collecting and
    returning them through Canvas.
  </p>

  <h2>1. What students do</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Open the lesson (e.g. <a href="../lessons/1.4.html">1.4</a>).</li>
    <li>Type their name in the bar at the bottom of the lesson once — it
      is remembered on that device for every other lesson.</li>
    <li>Answer the boxed questions as they work through the labs.
      Everything autosaves locally; nothing is lost if they close the
      tab.</li>
    <li>Click <strong>Download answers (.json)</strong> and upload that
      file to the Canvas assignment.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    The Dashboard also has a <strong>My work</strong> panel: one file
    backs up <em>all</em> answers plus progress, and can be restored on
    another machine (useful when lab machines get reimaged).
  </p>

  <h2>2. Set up the Canvas assignment (once per lesson)</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>In Canvas: <strong>Assignments &rarr; + Assignment</strong>.</li>
    <li>Submission Type: <strong>Online</strong>, check
      <strong>File Uploads</strong>. (Leave "Allowed File Types" blank so
      .json files upload cleanly.)</li>
    <li>Set points and the due date, then Publish.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>3. Read the submissions</h2>
  <p>
    Each submitted file is plain JSON you can open in any text editor,
    a browser, or <code>cat</code>. It looks like this:
  </p>
  <pre><code>{SAMPLE_JSON}</code></pre>
  <p>
    Because the question text rides along in the file, you can grade from
    the JSON alone — no need to open the site side by side. The id tells
    you the lesson, the block (bh = bug hunt, sc = socratic, dc = the
    Break It Down discussion), and the question number.
  </p>

  <h2>4. Return feedback</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>In the Canvas gradebook: <strong>SpeedGrader</strong> shows the
      .json as an attachment; open it in a new tab and read.</li>
    <li>Type the score and one line of feedback — the boxes are worth a
      few completion points, not essays; the labs themselves are where
      the learning shows.</li>
    <li>For a whole-class snapshot, <strong>Download Submissions</strong>
      on the assignment page and skim the files.</li>
  </ol>

  <div class="callout info">
    <strong>The MCQ clinic sets grade themselves.</strong> The three exam
    clinics (11.4a/b/c) now ship 48 real questions rendered from
    <code>src/data/mcq-bank.ts</code>. Students answer on the page, press
    <em>Grade this set</em>, and get a score plus the per-family error
    table the lesson asks them to build by hand. Those letters ride into
    the same answers <code>.json</code> you already collect — each
    question carries a hidden answer field, so no separate collection
    step is needed.
  </div>
  <p>
    Reflective answer boxes are still hand-graded: they are worth a few
    completion points, and the labs are where the learning shows. Only the
    MCQ sets are objective, and only they are autoscored.
  </p>

  <h2>5. MCQ bank — what to change and what it guards</h2>
  <table>
    <tr><th>To do this</th><th>Edit</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Add or reword a question</td><td><code>src/data/mcq-bank.ts</code> — the three clinic lessons, the key below, and <code>/data/mcq-keys.json</code> all regenerate from it</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Reorder a set</td><td><strong>Do not.</strong> Question ids are positional (<code>11.4a:mc:q3</code>), so reordering re-points students' saved answers and the keys' letter grades at the wrong question. Append, or rewrite a question in place</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Change an answer</td><td><code>answer:</code> is a 0-based index into <code>choices</code> — 0 is A</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Check nothing drifted</td><td><code>npm run verify</code> — check 6 fails if any key lacks a rendered question, any explanation is missing, any CED family is unknown, or the mixed sets fall outside the exam's big-idea ranges</td></tr>
  </table>

  <h3>Published weighting vs. what the sets actually contain</h3>
  <p>
    Sets 1 and 3 are the mixed practice sets and are held to the exam's
    published big-idea ranges. Set 2 is the targeted data-and-networks
    remediation set, so it deliberately spikes BI2 and BI4 and is
    <em>excluded</em> from that assertion — compare the two columns below
    and the reason is visible.
  </p>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Big idea</th><th>Families</th><th>Exam range</th><th>Sets 1 + 3 (mixed)</th><th>All 48</th></tr>
    {Object.entries(BIG_IDEA_RANGE).map(([bi, r]) => (
      <tr>
        <td><strong>{bi}</strong></td>
        <td>{r.families.join(', ')}</td>
        <td>{r.min}–{r.max}%</td>
        <td>{MIXED_SHARE[bi]}%</td>
        <td>{ALL_SHARE[bi]}%</td>
      </tr>
    ))}
  </table>

  <h2>6. Answer key (48 questions)</h2>
  <p>
    The same key the page grader fetches, printed here so you can see it at
    a glance while grading or reviewing a set in class. Generated from
    <code>src/data/mcq-bank.ts</code> at build time — never edit this
    table by hand.
  </p>
  {KEY_SETS.map(({ meta, rows }) => (
    <>
      <h3>{meta.label} — {rows.length} questions, {meta.minutes} min <span class="muted">({meta.focus})</span></h3>
      <table>
        <tr><th>#</th><th>CED</th><th>Ans</th><th>Correct choice</th><th>Trap</th></tr>
        {rows.map((r) => (
          <tr>
            <td>{r.n}</td>
            <td><code>{r.ced}</code></td>
            <td><strong>{r.answer}</strong></td>
            <td>{r.correct}</td>
            <td>{r.trap || '—'}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </table>
    </>
  ))}
  <p class="muted">
    Rationales for every question are in the answers
    <code>.json</code> that ships to students and in
    <code>src/data/mcq-bank.ts</code> itself; the grader reveals each one on
    screen when a student presses <em>Grade this set</em>.
  </p>

  <h2>7. The Create PT exception</h2>
  <p>
    College Board collaboration rules apply during the Create PT build
    days (April 13-27). Answer boxes on those lessons stay usable for
    <em>standup notes</em>, but the graded artifact is the program itself
    submitted to the AP Digital Portfolio, not Canvas. Keep Canvas for
    the process grade (commits, gates hit) and the exam clinics.
  </p>

  <h2>Site maintenance cheat-sheet</h2>
  <table>
    <tr><th>To do this</th><th>Run / edit this</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Add or re-date a lesson</td><td><code>src/data/curriculum.ts</code> (SEQUENCE) — the dashboard, chain, pace table, and PACING.md all follow automatically</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Rename a lesson title</td><td><code>src/data/lessons.ts</code> + the lesson page's h1</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Scaffold a new lesson page</td><td><code>npm run new-lesson -- 5.11</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Check site integrity</td><td><code>npm run build &amp;&amp; npm run verify</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Re-generate PACING.md / sitemap</td><td>happens inside every <code>npm run build</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Search index</td><td>Pagefind runs in build; nothing to maintain</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Add / reword an MCQ</td><td><code>src/data/mcq-bank.ts</code> — lessons, key page, and <code>/data/mcq-keys.json</code> all regenerate. Never reorder a set (ids are positional)</td></tr>
  </table>
</div>
</Base>
